  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nCurrent H5 analysis shows relatively high amplitude mid/upper\nlevel ridging over the Plains between upper troughing off the\nWest Coast and off the East Coast. At the surface, high pressure\nremains in place, resulting in mostly clear skies, light and\nvariable winds, and temperatures in the mid 70s as of 2 PM.\nClear skies and light winds will continue through the remainder\nof the afternoon hours and into tonight, with overnight lows in\nthe lower 50s across most of the CWA. The upper ridge axis\nshifts further east closer to our region tomorrow as the western\ntrough makes landfall across Southern California, with surface\nhigh pressure remaining overhead. This should increase\ntemperatures a few degrees for tomorrow afternoon, with clear\nskies and nearly calm winds continuing. \n\nAs we head into Friday and into the weekend, mid/upper ridging\nin remains in place overhead, but the western trough should move\ntoward the Four Corners region by Saturday night. This should\nhelp generate some increased cloud cover moving in from the west \nby Saturday and especially Sunday. Afternoon high temps should\nremain in the mid to upper 70s for Friday through Sunday,\nalthough the increased cloud cover overnight should keep\novernight lows a few degrees warmer. \n\nMost guidance wants to bring the aforementioned western trough \nand the associated cold front through the area Monday into \nTuesday, bringing in cooler temperatures and slight chance PoPs \n(15% to 20%). The main question at this time is just how much \ncolder will temperatures be, as the most recent NBM run shows \nthe 25th and 75th percentiles for MaxT on Monday at MCI as 63 \nand 73 degrees, respectively, and 58 and 73 degrees, \nrespectively, for Tuesday.",
